Rem ==================================================================
Rem neural.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: feed-forward neural nets
Rem
Rem One hidden layer, Tanh activation, trained by mini-batch SGD. A Net
Rem is a record of two weight matrices and two bias vectors; a GRADIENT
Rem HAS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE NETWORK, so the same record represents
Rem both and accumulate / average / apply-update all fall out of NetAdd
Rem and NetScale — no zeroing loops, no mutable scratch arrays. The
Rem same identity carries MOMENTUM: a velocity is a Net too.
Rem
Rem Two output heads, one gradient. Regress reads the linear output
Rem as-is and fits squared error; Classify reads it through Softmax and
Rem fits cross-entropy. Those two look like different networks in most
Rem textbooks, but the error signal at the output pre-activation is the
Rem same expression for both — (what the head predicts) minus (the
Rem target) — so NetGradAt has exactly one line that knows which head
Rem it is serving, and everything downstream is shared.
Rem
Rem Two ways in, matching that split:
Rem   * NetTrain — regression, scalar targets, plain SGD. Unchanged.
Rem   * NetFit   — either head, vector targets, momentum, driven by a
Rem     Plan record so the hyperparameters travel as one value.
Rem
Rem Scale: like stats.shoddy this is classroom-sized — hundreds of rows
Rem and a few thousand epochs through immutable arrays, not bulk
Rem training.
Rem
Rem Impure edges (the only two, both via random.shoddy): NetNew draws
Rem its initial weights from Rnd, and NetTrain shuffles each epoch. A
Rem caller wanting reproducible runs calls the Seed builtin once, at
Rem the top of Main — the same edge random.shoddy documents.
Rem
Rem Serialization, two formats over one canonical weight order (input-
Rem hidden weights input-major, hidden biases, hidden-output weights
Rem hidden-major, output biases):
Rem   * NetSave/NetLoad — text, one number per line, so weight files
Rem     interchange with the C# reference program. Str writes 10
Rem     significant digits, so a round trip reproduces predictions to
Rem     ~1e-10 relative, not to the last bit.
Rem   * NetSaveBin/NetLoadBin — binary over recio, a self-describing
Rem     header (magic, ni, nh, no) then raw 8-byte IEEE doubles, so a
Rem     round trip is BIT-EXACT and loading needs no architecture
Rem     arguments.
Rem   * ModelSave/ModelLoad — the same binary discipline over a Model:
Rem     the weights PLUS the head and the input Scaler. A Net alone is
Rem     not a predictor, because whoever trained it also decided how to
Rem     centre and scale the inputs, and a caller who reconstructs that
Rem     decision by hand at prediction time is one edit away from a
Rem     silently wrong answer. ModelPredict takes raw inputs and applies
Rem     the scaling the file remembers, so the two cannot drift apart.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "matrix.shoddy"
Include "random.shoddy"
Include "file.shoddy"
Include "recio.shoddy"

Rem W1 rows are hidden neurons: MatVec(W1, x) is the hidden pre-
Rem activation in one word. NOTE this is the TRANSPOSE of the C#
Rem reference's ihWeights (input-major) layout; NetMatSerial bridges.
Type Net
    W1 As Matrix              Rem nh x ni
    B1 As Array Of Number     Rem nh
    W2 As Matrix              Rem no x nh
    B2 As Array Of Number     Rem no (1 for regression)

Rem A forward pass keeps its post-tanh hidden activations — backprop
Rem needs them, and NetOut throws them away for free.
Type NetEval
    Hidden As Array Of Number
    Output As Array Of Number

Rem ---- the gradient algebra: same shape, two words -------------------

Def NetAdd(a As Net, b As Net) As Net
    Net(MatAdd(W1(a), W1(b)), VAdd(B1(a), B1(b)), MatAdd(W2(a), W2(b)), VAdd(B2(a), B2(b)))

Def NetScale(n As Net, s As Number) As Net
    Net(MatScale(W1(n), s), VScale(B1(n), s), MatScale(W2(n), s), VScale(B2(n), s))

Rem ---- construction (IMPURE: Rnd; Seed first for reproducibility) ----

Def NetRandArray(n As Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(k) => RandomRange(-0.01, 0.01)))

Def NetNew(ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Net(Matrix(nh, ni, NetRandArray(nh * ni)), NetRandArray(nh), Matrix(no, nh, NetRandArray(no * nh)), NetRandArray(no))

Rem Xavier/Glorot initialization: draw from ±sqrt(6 / (fanIn + fanOut)),
Rem so the spread narrows as a layer widens. NetNew's fixed ±0.01 is
Rem fine for the handful of inputs a regression demo has, but on a layer
Rem with a hundred-odd inputs it starts every hidden unit within a
Rem whisker of zero — deep in the linear part of Tanh, with every output
Rem near-identical, and a long slow crawl before the units differentiate
Rem at all. Biases start at zero: they need no symmetry breaking, since
Rem the weights have already provided it.
Def NetXavier(rows As Number, cols As Number) As Array Of Number
    Let b = Sqr(6 / (rows + cols))
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, rows * cols), Fn(k) => RandomRange(Negate(b), b)))

Def NetNewScaled(ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Net(Matrix(nh, ni, NetXavier(nh, ni)), Dim(nh, 0), Matrix(no, nh, NetXavier(no, nh)), Dim(no, 0))

Rem A net of the same shape, all zeros — the starting velocity for
Rem momentum. Scaling by zero is the shortest way to say "this shape".
Def NetZero(n As Net) As Net
    NetScale(n, 0)

Rem ---- forward -------------------------------------------------------

Def NetEvalAt(n As Net, x As Array Of Number) As NetEval
    Let h = Map(VAdd(MatVec(W1(n), x), B1(n)), Tanh)
    NetEval(h, VAdd(MatVec(W2(n), h), B2(n)))

Def NetOut(n As Net, x As Array Of Number) As Number
    First(Output(NetEvalAt(n, x)))

Rem ---- output heads --------------------------------------------------

Rem The output layer is linear either way; the HEAD says how to read it.
Rem Regress takes it as-is; Classify pushes it through Softmax to get
Rem probabilities that sum to 1.
Type Head = Regress | Classify

Rem Subtracting the largest logit first cannot change the answer —
Rem softmax is shift-invariant — and it keeps Exp away from overflow.
Def Softmax(zs As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    Let m = Fold(zs, Nth(zs, 1), Max)
    Let es = Map(zs, Fn(z) => Exp(z - m))
    VScale(es, 1 / Fold(es, 0, +))

Def HeadOut(head As Head, z As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    Select Case head
        Case Classify
            Softmax(z)
        Case Else
            z

Rem The whole output vector, read through the head. NetOut above is the
Rem scalar shortcut for the one-output regression case.
Def NetPredict(n As Net, head As Head, x As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    HeadOut(head, Output(NetEvalAt(n, x)))

Def Argmax(zs As Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(Range(2, Length(zs)), 1, Fn(best, k) => Ifte(Nth(zs, k) > Nth(zs, best), k, best))

Rem Softmax is monotonic, so the winning class is simply the largest
Rem logit — the exponentials never have to be computed to find it.
Def NetClassOf(n As Net, x As Array Of Number) As Number
    Argmax(Output(NetEvalAt(n, x)))

Rem A class index as a target vector: 1 in position k, 0 elsewhere.
Rem Kron is the equality indicator, so a one-hot is Kron along the row.
Def NetOneHot(k As Number, n As Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Kron(i, k)))

Rem ---- backward ------------------------------------------------------

Rem One example's gradient. oSig = (what the head predicts) - target;
Rem hSig = W2^T oSig  ⊙  (1 - h^2), with the tanh derivative written
Rem (1-h)(1+h); the weight gradients are outer products of a signal with
Rem the layer's input.
Rem
Rem THE ONE LINE THAT KNOWS THE HEAD is oSig. For identity output under
Rem squared error and for softmax output under cross-entropy the error
Rem signal at the output pre-activation is the same difference, which is
Rem why one backward pass serves regression and classification alike.
Rem Both conventions omit the factor of 2 (squared error is really HALF
Rem squared error here), absorbed by the learning rate — see the
Rem finite-difference tests in tst/neuraltest.shoddy, which difference
Rem that same half loss.
Def NetGradAt(n As Net, head As Head, x As Array Of Number, ys As Array Of Number) As Net
    Let e = NetEvalAt(n, x)
    Let h = Hidden(e)
    Let oSig = VSub(HeadOut(head, Output(e)), ys)
    Let hSig = VMul(MatVec(Transp(W2(n)), oSig), Map(h, Fn(v) => (1 - v) * (1 + v)))
    Net(Outer(hSig, x), hSig, Outer(oSig, h), oSig)

Rem The scalar-target regression form, kept because NetTrain and every
Rem existing caller speak it. It is a wrapper, not a second copy: the
Rem backward pass above is the only one in the machine, so the two
Rem cannot drift. VSub is dimension-checked, which makes a scalar target
Rem against a multi-output net an error here rather than a silent
Rem broadcast of one number onto every output.
Def NetGrad(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number) As Net
    NetGradAt(n, Regress(), x, ToArray({ y }))

Rem One mini-batch step: average the per-example gradients, descend.
Def NetStep(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, lr As Number) As Net
    Let grads = ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetGrad(n, x, y))
    Let avg = NetScale(Fold(Rest(grads), First(grads), NetAdd), 1 / Length(grads))
    NetAdd(n, NetScale(avg, Negate(lr)))

Rem ---- training ------------------------------------------------------

Def NetBatch(net As Net, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Number, perm As Array Of Number, b As Number, batSize As Number, lr As Number) As Net
    Let idx = Map(Range((b - 1) * batSize + 1, b * batSize), Fn(k) => Nth(perm, k))
    NetStep(net, Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(xa, i)), Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(ya, i)), lr)

Rem One epoch: shuffle the row indices (IMPURE: Rnd), then tile the
Rem whole permutation with Floor(n / batSize) consecutive chunks —
Rem the remainder rows are dropped, matching the C# int division.
Def NetEpoch(net As Net, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Number, lr As Number, batSize As Number) As Net
    Let perm = ToArray(Shuffle(Range(1, Length(ya))))
    Fold(Range(1, Floor(Length(ya) / batSize)), net, Fn(nn, b) => NetBatch(nn, xa, ya, perm, b, batSize, lr))

Def NetReport(net As Net, epoch As Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Net
    Call(report, epoch, net)
    net

Rem Fold over epochs. report is called with (epoch, net) after EVERY
Rem epoch — the caller decides when to print and pay for metrics, so
Rem both the effect and the cost stay at the mill's edge.
Def NetTrain(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, lr As Number, batSize As Number, epochs As Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Net
    Let xa = ToArray(xs)
    Let ya = ToArray(ys)
    Fold(Range(1, epochs), n, Fn(net, epoch) => NetReport(NetEpoch(net, xa, ya, lr, batSize), epoch, report))

Rem ---- training, the general form ------------------------------------

Rem Everything NetFit needs to know that is not the data, carried as one
Rem value so a training run can be named, printed, and passed around
Rem rather than smeared across seven arguments. Moment is the momentum
Rem coefficient: 0 reduces this to the plain descent NetTrain does.
Type Plan
    Task   As Head
    Rate   As Number
    Moment As Number
    Batch  As Number
    Epochs As Number

Def ClassPlan(lr As Number, mu As Number, batch As Number, epochs As Number) As Plan
    Plan(Classify(), lr, mu, batch, epochs)

Def RegressPlan(lr As Number, mu As Number, batch As Number, epochs As Number) As Plan
    Plan(Regress(), lr, mu, batch, epochs)

Rem The state carried through training is (net, velocity) — Fst and Snd
Rem of a Pair. Momentum keeps a running step rather than recomputing one
Rem from scratch each batch: v = mu*v - lr*grad, then the net moves by v.
Rem Consecutive batches that agree about the direction accumulate speed;
Rem ones that disagree cancel. A velocity is a Net, so the same NetAdd
Rem and NetScale that average the gradients also do this.
Def NetStepAt(p As Pair, pl As Plan, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number) As Pair
    Let n = Fst(p)
    Let grads = ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetGradAt(n, Task(pl), x, y))
    Let avg = NetScale(Fold(Rest(grads), First(grads), NetAdd), 1 / Length(grads))
    Let v = NetAdd(NetScale(Snd(p), Moment(pl)), NetScale(avg, Negate(Rate(pl))))
    Pair(NetAdd(n, v), v)

Def NetBatchAt(p As Pair, pl As Plan, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Array Of Number, perm As Array Of Number, b As Number) As Pair
    Let idx = Map(Range((b - 1) * Batch(pl) + 1, b * Batch(pl)), Fn(k) => Nth(perm, k))
    NetStepAt(p, pl, Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(xa, i)), Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(ya, i)))

Rem One epoch: shuffle the row indices (IMPURE: Rnd), then tile the whole
Rem permutation with Floor(n / batSize) consecutive chunks — the same
Rem remainder-dropping NetEpoch does, and harmless for the same reason,
Rem since the next epoch reshuffles and the dropped rows are not the
Rem same ones twice.
Def NetEpochAt(p As Pair, pl As Plan, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Array Of Number) As Pair
    Let perm = ToArray(Shuffle(Range(1, Length(ya))))
    Fold(Range(1, Floor(Length(ya) / Batch(pl))), p, Fn(q, b) => NetBatchAt(q, pl, xa, ya, perm, b))

Def NetFitReport(p As Pair, epoch As Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Pair
    Call(report, epoch, Fst(p))
    p

Rem Fold over epochs, threading net and velocity. report is called with
Rem (epoch, net) after EVERY epoch — the caller decides when to print and
Rem pay for metrics, so both the effect and the cost stay at the mill's
Rem edge. The velocity starts at zero and is discarded at the end: it is
Rem scaffolding for the descent, not part of the model.
Def NetFit(n As Net, pl As Plan, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Net
    Let xa = ToArray(xs)
    Let ya = ToArray(ys)
    Fst(Fold(Range(1, Epochs(pl)), Pair(n, NetZero(n)), Fn(p, epoch) => NetFitReport(NetEpochAt(p, pl, xa, ya), epoch, report)))

Rem ---- metrics -------------------------------------------------------

Def NetSqErr(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number) As Number
    Let d = NetOut(n, x) - y
    d * d

Def NetMse(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetSqErr(n, x, y)), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem The fraction of rows predicted within pctClose of the actual value
Rem (|pred - actual| < pctClose * |actual|) — percent-close accuracy.
Def NetClose(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number, pctClose As Number) As Number
    If Abs(NetOut(n, x) - y) < Abs(pctClose * y) Then
        1
    Else
        0

Def NetAccuracy(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, pctClose As Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetClose(n, x, y, pctClose)), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem ---- metrics for classification ------------------------------------

Rem Percent-close accuracy above is meaningless once the outputs are
Rem class scores, and so is MSE: these are the two a classifier is
Rem actually judged by. Targets are one-hot vectors, as NetFit takes.

Rem Cross-entropy: the negative log of the probability the net gave to
Rem the true class. Zero when it was certain and right, and it climbs
Rem without bound as the probability it assigned to the truth goes to
Rem zero — which is why it has to be floored before Log sees it, since
Rem a confident enough mistake underflows to exactly 0 and Log(0) has no
Rem answer to give.
Def NetFloor() As Number
    0.000000000000001

Def NetLogLossAt(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, ys As Array Of Number) As Number
    Let p = Softmax(Output(NetEvalAt(n, x)))
    Negate(Fold(ZipWith(ys, p, Fn(t, q) => t * Log(Max(q, NetFloor()))), 0, +))

Def NetLogLoss(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetLogLossAt(n, x, y)), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem Winner-takes-all accuracy: the fraction of rows whose largest output
Rem sits in the same position as the one-hot target's 1.
Def NetClassAccuracy(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Array Of Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => Kron(NetClassOf(n, x), Argmax(y))), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem ---- save / load (effectful, like all file words) ------------------

Rem The C# demo serializes ih weights INPUT-major (for i in inputs, for
Rem j in hidden) — that is column-major over our W1, whose layout is
Rem the transpose of the C# ihWeights. Transp-then-flat-cells walks
Rem exactly that order (and hidden-major over W2, likewise). This is
Rem where a silent transpose bug would live; the finite-difference and
Rem round-trip tests in tst/neuraltest.shoddy guard it.
Def NetMatSerial(m As Matrix) As List Of Number
    ToList(Cells(Transp(m)))

Def NetWeightList(n As Net) As List Of Number
    Concat(Concat(NetMatSerial(W1(n)), ToList(B1(n))), Concat(NetMatSerial(W2(n)), ToList(B2(n))))

Def NetSave(path As String, n As Net)
    WriteLines(path, Map(NetWeightList(n), Str))

Def NetSlice(ws As Array Of Number, start As Number, count As Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(start, start + count - 1), Fn(k) => Nth(ws, k)))

Rem The inverse of NetWeightList: a Net from a flat weight array in the
Rem serialization order above.
Def NetFromList(ws As Array Of Number, ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Let inW = Transp(Matrix(ni, nh, NetSlice(ws, 1, ni * nh)))
    Let inB = NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + 1, nh)
    Let outW = Transp(Matrix(nh, no, NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + nh + 1, nh * no)))
    Let outB = NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + nh + nh * no + 1, no)
    Net(inW, inB, outW, outB)

Def NetLoad(path As String, ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Let ws = ToArray(Map(ReadLines(path), Val))
    If Length(ws) <> ni * nh + nh + nh * no + no Then
        Error("NETLOAD: WRONG NUMBER OF WEIGHTS FOR " & Str(ni) & "-" & Str(nh) & "-" & Str(no))
    Else
        NetFromList(ws, ni, nh, no)

Rem ---- binary save / load (over recio; bit-exact) --------------------

Rem A model file is a stream of 8-byte doubles: a 4-double header —
Rem the magic (1986, backpropagation's year), then ni, nh, no — then
Rem the weights in the canonical order above. Every double is read and
Rem written through recio's PutRec/GetRec at an explicit 1-based slot,
Rem so nothing leans on sequential file-position state.

Def NetBinMagic() As Number
    1986

Def NetPutAt(f As Number, k As Number, v As Number)
    PutRec(f, k, 8, PutNum, v)

Def NetGetAt(f As Number, k As Number) As Number
    GetRec(f, k, 8, GetNum)

Def NetSaveBin(path As String, n As Net)
    If FileExists(path) Then
        DeleteFile(path)
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    NetPutAt(f, 1, NetBinMagic())
    NetPutAt(f, 2, Cols(W1(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 3, Rows(W1(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 4, Rows(W2(n)))
    Let ws = ToArray(NetWeightList(n))
    Each(Range(1, Length(ws)), Fn(k) => NetPutAt(f, k + 4, Nth(ws, k)))
    BClose(f)

Rem Self-describing: the header carries the architecture, so no
Rem ni/nh/no arguments. The magic and the exact byte size are checked
Rem before any weight is trusted.
Def NetLoadBin(path As String) As Net
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    If BSize(f) < 32 Or NetGetAt(f, 1) <> NetBinMagic() Then
        Error("NETLOADBIN: NOT A NEURAL MODEL FILE: " & path)
    Let ni = NetGetAt(f, 2)
    Let nh = NetGetAt(f, 3)
    Let no = NetGetAt(f, 4)
    Let count = ni * nh + nh + nh * no + no
    If BSize(f) <> 8 * (4 + count) Then
        Error("NETLOADBIN: WRONG SIZE FOR " & Str(ni) & "-" & Str(nh) & "-" & Str(no) & " MODEL: " & path)
    Let ws = ToArray(Map(Range(1, count), Fn(k) => NetGetAt(f, k + 4)))
    BClose(f)
    NetFromList(ws, ni, nh, no)

Rem ---- input scaling -------------------------------------------------

Rem Tanh saturates, so an input column measured in thousands swamps one
Rem measured in tenths and the network spends its first few hundred
Rem epochs undoing the units. A Scaler centres each column on its mean
Rem and divides by its standard deviation, putting every input on the
Rem same footing.
Rem
Rem Center and Spread rather than Mean and StdDev: those two words are
Rem already declared by stats.shoddy, and a program that includes both
Rem machines would be refused for declaring a name twice.
Type Scaler
    Center As Array Of Number
    Spread As Array Of Number

Def ScalerCol(xs As List Of Array Of Number, j As Number) As List Of Number
    Map(xs, Fn(x) => Nth(x, j))

Def ScalerMean(vs As List Of Number) As Number
    Fold(vs, 0, +) / Length(vs)

Rem Population spread (divide by n), because this is a description of
Rem the training columns rather than an estimate about a wider
Rem population. A column that never varies gets a spread of 1, not 0:
Rem it carries no information either way, and 1 maps it to a harmless
Rem constant 0 instead of dividing by zero.
Def ScalerSd(vs As List Of Number) As Number
    Let m = ScalerMean(vs)
    Let s = Sqr(Fold(Map(vs, Fn(v) => (v - m) * (v - m)), 0, +) / Length(vs))
    If s = 0 Then
        1
    Else
        s

Def ScalerFit(xs As List Of Array Of Number) As Scaler
    Let ni = Length(First(xs))
    Scaler(ToArray(Map(Range(1, ni), Fn(j) => ScalerMean(ScalerCol(xs, j)))),
           ToArray(Map(Range(1, ni), Fn(j) => ScalerSd(ScalerCol(xs, j)))))

Def ScalerApply(s As Scaler, x As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, Length(x)), Fn(j) => (Nth(x, j) - Nth(Center(s), j)) / Nth(Spread(s), j)))

Rem The identity scaler, for inputs that are already comparable.
Def ScalerNone(ni As Number) As Scaler
    Scaler(Dim(ni, 0), Dim(ni, 1))

Rem ---- the model: weights, head, and scaling as one artifact ---------

Rem A Net on its own is not enough to predict with. Whoever trained it
Rem also chose a head and a scaling of the inputs, and a caller who
Rem rebuilds those by hand is maintaining a second copy of a decision —
Rem the classic way to end up serving a model different inputs than the
Rem ones it was fitted on, with nothing to warn you. A Model carries all
Rem three, ModelPredict takes RAW inputs, and the drift has nowhere to
Rem happen.
Type Model
    Weights As Net
    Task    As Head
    Prep    As Scaler

Def ModelPredict(m As Model, x As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    NetPredict(Weights(m), Task(m), ScalerApply(Prep(m), x))

Def ModelClassOf(m As Model, x As Array Of Number) As Number
    Argmax(ModelPredict(m, x))

Rem ---- model save / load (over recio; bit-exact) ---------------------

Rem A model file is a stream of 8-byte doubles: a 5-double header — the
Rem magic, then ni, nh, no, then the head as 0 or 1 — followed by the
Rem weights in NetWeightList's canonical order, then the scaler's ni
Rem centres and ni spreads. Self-describing like NetSaveBin, so
Rem ModelLoad takes no architecture arguments.
Rem
Rem A DIFFERENT magic from NetSaveBin's 1986, and a separate pair of
Rem words, so the two formats can never be mistaken for one another:
Rem files already written by NetSaveBin stay readable by exactly the
Rem word that wrote them.
Def ModelBinMagic() As Number
    1987

Def HeadCode(h As Head) As Number
    Select Case h
        Case Classify
            1
        Case Else
            0

Def HeadOfCode(c As Number) As Head
    If c = 1 Then
        Classify()
    Else
        Regress()

Def ModelSave(path As String, m As Model)
    If FileExists(path) Then
        DeleteFile(path)
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    Let n = Weights(m)
    Let ni = Cols(W1(n))
    NetPutAt(f, 1, ModelBinMagic())
    NetPutAt(f, 2, ni)
    NetPutAt(f, 3, Rows(W1(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 4, Rows(W2(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 5, HeadCode(Task(m)))
    Let ws = ToArray(NetWeightList(n))
    Each(Range(1, Length(ws)), Fn(k) => NetPutAt(f, k + 5, Nth(ws, k)))
    Each(Range(1, ni), Fn(k) => NetPutAt(f, k + 5 + Length(ws), Nth(Center(Prep(m)), k)))
    Each(Range(1, ni), Fn(k) => NetPutAt(f, k + 5 + Length(ws) + ni, Nth(Spread(Prep(m)), k)))
    BClose(f)

Def ModelLoad(path As String) As Model
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    If BSize(f) < 40 Or NetGetAt(f, 1) <> ModelBinMagic() Then
        Error("MODELLOAD: NOT A NEURAL MODEL FILE: " & path)
    Let ni = NetGetAt(f, 2)
    Let nh = NetGetAt(f, 3)
    Let no = NetGetAt(f, 4)
    Let head = HeadOfCode(NetGetAt(f, 5))
    Let count = ni * nh + nh + nh * no + no
    If BSize(f) <> 8 * (5 + count + 2 * ni) Then
        Error("MODELLOAD: WRONG SIZE FOR " & Str(ni) & "-" & Str(nh) & "-" & Str(no) & " MODEL: " & path)
    Let ws = ToArray(Map(Range(1, count), Fn(k) => NetGetAt(f, k + 5)))
    Let ctr = ToArray(Map(Range(1, ni), Fn(k) => NetGetAt(f, k + 5 + count)))
    Let spr = ToArray(Map(Range(1, ni), Fn(k) => NetGetAt(f, k + 5 + count + ni)))
    BClose(f)
    Model(NetFromList(ws, ni, nh, no), head, Scaler(ctr, spr))
